       uts_namespaces - overview of Linux UTS namespaces

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       UTS  namespaces  provide isolation of two system identifiers: the host‐
       name and the NIS domain name.  These identifiers are set using sseetthhoosstt‐‐
       nnaammee(2) and sseettddoommaaiinnnnaammee(2), and  can  be  retrieved  using  uunnaammee(2),
       ggeetthhoossttnnaammee(2),  and  ggeettddoommaaiinnnnaammee(2).   Changes made to these identi‐
       fiers are visible to all other processes in the same UTS namespace, but
       are not visible to processes in other UTS namespaces.

       When a process creates a new UTS namespace using cclloonnee(2) or uunnsshhaarree(2)
       with the CCLLOONNEE__NNEEWWUUTTSS flag, the hostname and domain name of the new UTS
       namespace are copied from the corresponding values in the caller’s  UTS
       namespace.

       Use  of  UTS  namespaces  requires a kernel that is configured with the
       CCOONNFFIIGG__UUTTSS__NNSS option.

